{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Chasing Cherry Blossoms in Kyoto**\n\nLast spring, I finally made the trip to Japan, a dream deferred for far too long. Stepping off the Shinkansen in Kyoto felt like entering a different world where ancient tradition and hyper-modernity coexist in perfect harmony.\n\nMy first stop was the iconic Fushimi Inari Taisha. Walking through the thousands of vermilion torii gates winding up the mountain was surreal. The air smelled of cedar and incense, and the only sounds were the rustling leaves and distant temple bells. It was a meditative experience that instantly slowed my frantic pace.\n\nThe next day, I got lost in the Arashiyama Bamboo Grove. The stalks towered overhead, filtering the sunlight into a soft, green glow. Later, I sat by the Kamo River, watching locals fly kites and couples enjoy picnics under blooming sakura trees. The petals drifted down like pink snow, creating a scene straight out of a painting.\n\nOf course, no trip to Japan is complete without the food. From steaming bowls of rich tonkotsu ramen in narrow alleyways to delicate kaiseki dinners served on lacquerware, every meal was an event. I even tried my hand at a tea ceremony, learning the precise movements required to whisk matcha into a frothy masterpiece.\n\nWhat struck me most wasn't just the sights, but the Omotenashi—the wholehearted hospitality. Every shopkeeper, train conductor, and stranger offered a bow and a smile that made me feel genuinely welcome.\n\nLeaving Japan was bittersweet. I returned home with a camera full of photos and a heart full of memories, already planning my next adventure to explore Tokyo's neon streets or the snowy peaks of Hokkaido. Until then, the spirit of Japan remains with me.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.044555947}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.11277496}, {"token": "asing", "logprob": -1.1127625}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob":